<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Search for quantum black holes in lepton+jet final states using
proton–proton collisions at <math id="m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{s}=13.6\penalty\ \textrm{TeV}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mi>s</mi></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>13.6</mn><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>TeV</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{s}=13.6\penalty\ \textrm{TeV}</annotation></semantics></math> with the ATLAS detector</h1>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">A search for quantum black holes in electron+jet or muon+jet final states with high invariant mass is performed. The analysis uses data from <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{s}=13.6\penalty\ \textrm{TeV}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mi>s</mi></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>13.6</mn><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>TeV</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{s}=13.6\penalty\ \textrm{TeV}</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="pp" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">pp</annotation></semantics></math> collisions recorded by the ATLAS detector between 2022 and 2024 during Run 3 of the Large Hadron Collider, corresponding to an integrated luminosity of <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="164\penalty\ \mathrm{fb}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>164</mn><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>fb</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">164\penalty\ \mathrm{fb}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. This search is strongly motivated by a dramatic increase of the production cross-section by up to an order of magnitude for the highest masses considered, thanks to the small increase of <math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.6\penalty\ \textrm{TeV}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.6</mn><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>TeV</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.6\penalty\ \textrm{TeV}</annotation></semantics></math> in centre-of-mass energy between Run 2 and Run 3. No significant excess above the Standard Model background is observed, and 95% CL upper limits are set on the production cross-section times branching ratio in several benchmark models, reaching a mass scale of <math id="abstract1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9.4\penalty\ \textrm{TeV}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>9.4</mn><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>TeV</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9.4\penalty\ \textrm{TeV}</annotation></semantics></math>. These represent the strongest exclusion limits to date on quantum black hole production.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The hierarchy problem—the immense gulf between the electroweak scale and Planck scale—may be resolved by theories with extra dimensions (EDs) that lower the fundamental <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional scale of gravity, <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\textrm{D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>D</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\textrm{D}}</annotation></semantics></math>, to the TeV range.
In the Arkani-Hamed, Dimopoulos, and Dvali (ADD) model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>, gravity propagates through <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> flat EDs (where <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=4+n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=4+n</annotation></semantics></math>), while Standard Model (SM) fields are restricted to
four-dimensional space-time. Alternatively, the Randall–Sundrum (RS) model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> employs one warped extra dimension to generate this scale hierarchy.
These theories predict a plethora of new phenomena, some of which are accessible at the Large Hadron Collider (LHC).
One such prediction is the production of quantum black holes (QBHs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> at threshold masses <math id="S1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\textrm{th}}\sim M_{\textrm{D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>th</mtext></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>D</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\textrm{th}}\sim M_{\textrm{D}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In this benchmark framework, it is postulated that the QBH parton–parton cross-section follows a power-law increase with partonic centre-of-mass energy, with the power determined by the number of extra dimensions.
For higher proton-beam energy, the suppression of the partonic cross-section arising from the parton distribution functions (PDF) at high Bjorken-<math id="S1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> (the fraction of the proton’s momentum carried by the interacting parton) is reduced, resulting in a significant increase in the QBH production cross-section, <math id="S1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\textrm{QBH}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mtext>QBH</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\textrm{QBH}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Unlike semi-classical black holes, which decay thermally via Hawking radiation into high-multiplicity final states <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, these QBHs decay into two-particle final states, where lepton- and baryon-number conservation may be violated.
This search focuses on diquark production of QBHs that can decay to lepton+antiquark <math id="S1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\ell+\bar{q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\ell+\bar{q})</annotation></semantics></math> final states:</p>
