<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where the superscript indicates the QBH electric charge and the processes are sorted by descending <math id="S1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\textrm{QBH}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mtext>QBH</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\textrm{QBH}}</annotation></semantics></math>, left-to-right.
The notation <math id="S1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> marks the up-type and down-type quarks.
The branching ratios for the processes ordered as in Eq. (<a href="#S1.E1" title='Equation 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Search for quantum black holes in lepton+jet final states using proton–proton collisions at √𝑠=13.6⁢"TeV" with the ATLAS detector' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) are 11%, 5.6% and 6.7%, to each lepton flavour <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A key feature that sets this model apart from virtually all other new-physics scenarios, and motivates this search already with a partial run dataset, is the production cross-section’s strong dependence on the proton–proton centre-of-mass energy, <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mi>s</mi></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{s}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This dependence causes the increase in <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mi>s</mi></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{s}</annotation></semantics></math> from Run 2 of the LHC (<math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="13\penalty\ \textrm{TeV}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>13</mn><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>TeV</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">13\penalty\ \textrm{TeV}</annotation></semantics></math>) to Run 3 (<math id="S1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="13.6\penalty\ \textrm{TeV}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>13.6</mn><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>TeV</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">13.6\penalty\ \textrm{TeV}</annotation></semantics></math>) to enhance <math id="S1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\textrm{QBH}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mtext>QBH</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\textrm{QBH}}</annotation></semantics></math> by approximately <math id="S1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>100</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100\%</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\textrm{th}}=6\penalty\ \textrm{TeV}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>th</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>TeV</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\textrm{th}}=6\penalty\ \textrm{TeV}</annotation></semantics></math> up to about <math id="S1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1000\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1000</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1000\%</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\textrm{th}}=10.5\penalty\ \textrm{TeV}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>th</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>10.5</mn><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>TeV</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\textrm{th}}=10.5\penalty\ \textrm{TeV}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This analysis succeeds the previous ATLAS search performed at <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{s}=13\penalty\ \textrm{TeV}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mi>s</mi></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>13</mn><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>TeV</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{s}=13\penalty\ \textrm{TeV}</annotation></semantics></math> using the full <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="140\penalty\ \textrm{fb}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>140</mn><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mtext>fb</mtext><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">140\penalty\ \textrm{fb}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> Run 2 dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>.
The same search was done by ATLAS at <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{s}=8\penalty\ \textrm{TeV}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mi>s</mi></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>TeV</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{s}=8\penalty\ \textrm{TeV}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20.3\penalty\ \textrm{fb}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>20.3</mn><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mtext>fb</mtext><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20.3\penalty\ \textrm{fb}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> of data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>.
Complementary searches were also performed in dijet, dilepton and photon+jet final states by ATLAS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite> and CMS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>.
In comparison with the other final states, the lepton+jet ones benefit from significantly lower backgrounds, which compensates for their smaller cross-section times branching ratio.
Lepton+jet final states are of course not unique to QBH production, and other searches probing similar regions of phase space do not necessarily provide an explicit interpretation in terms of QBHs.
For example, ATLAS has published a lepton+jet search for leptoquarks with a combination of the full Run 2 dataset and a partial (<math id="S1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="56\penalty\ \textrm{fb}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>56</mn><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mtext>fb</mtext><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">56\penalty\ \textrm{fb}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>) Run 3 dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The present search uses <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="164\penalty\ \textrm{fb}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>164</mn><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mtext>fb</mtext><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">164\penalty\ \textrm{fb}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{s}=13.6\penalty\ \textrm{TeV}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mi>s</mi></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>13.6</mn><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>TeV</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{s}=13.6\penalty\ \textrm{TeV}</annotation></semantics></math> proton–proton collision data, recorded by the ATLAS detector during the 2022–2024 period of Run 3. Even with a Run 3 dataset similar in size to the full Run 2 dataset, this analysis yields considerably higher QBH exclusion limits thanks to the significant increase in <math id="S1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\textrm{QBH}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mtext>QBH</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\textrm{QBH}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Electron+jet and muon+jet final states with an invariant mass above <math id="S1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\penalty\ \textrm{TeV}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>TeV</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\penalty\ \textrm{TeV}</annotation></semantics></math> are targeted separately in this search.
Explicit contributions from QBH decays with <math id="S1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>-leptons are not considered, since they are expected to be negligible compared to those from the other two lepton+jet final states, owing to the <math id="S1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\rightarrow\ell\bar{\nu}_{\ell}\nu_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ν</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\rightarrow\ell\bar{\nu}_{\ell}\nu_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> branching ratio and the softer electron and muon transverse momentum (<math id="S1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\textrm{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>T</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\textrm{T}}</annotation></semantics></math>) spectra due to the presence of neutrinos in <math id="S1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> decays.
The main background processes include <math id="S1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>+ ​jets (<math id="S1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V=W,Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V=W,Z</annotation></semantics></math>), <math id="S1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="VV" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">VV</annotation></semantics></math> and events with top quarks.
These are modelled using Monte Carlo (MC) simulations.
Events containing non-prompt electrons, or electrons from photon conversions or jets misidentified as electrons, collectively referred to as <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“fakes”</span>, are estimated using data-driven techniques.
This is the dominant background in the electron channel, while <math id="S1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>+ ​jets production contributes the most background in the muon channel.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>ATLAS detector</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The ATLAS experiment <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> at the LHC is a multipurpose particle detector
with a forward–backward symmetric cylindrical geometry and a near <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4\pi</annotation></semantics></math> coverage in
solid angle.<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
            
            
            
            
            
            
            
          ATLAS uses a right-handed coordinate system with its
origin at the nominal interaction point (IP) in the centre of
the detector and the <math id="footnote1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math>-axis along the beam pipe. The <math id="footnote1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>-axis
points from the IP to the centre of the LHC ring, and the
<math id="footnote1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>-axis points upwards. Polar coordinates (<math id="footnote1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="footnote1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math>) are used in
the transverse plane, <math id="footnote1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> being the azimuthal angle around
the <math id="footnote1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> axis. The pseudorapidity is defined in terms of the
polar angle <math id="footnote1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="footnote1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta=-\ln\tan(\theta/2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>tan</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta=-\ln\tan(\theta/2)</annotation></semantics></math> and is equal to the
rapidity <math id="footnote1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=(1/2)\ln[(E+p_{z})/(E-p_{z})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>z</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>z</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=(1/2)\ln[(E+p_{z})/(E-p_{z})]</annotation></semantics></math> in the relativistic limit. Angular distance is measured in units of
<math id="footnote1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta R\equiv\sqrt{(\Delta y)^{2}+(\Delta\phi)^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo>≡</mo><msqrt><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta R\equiv\sqrt{(\Delta y)^{2}+(\Delta\phi)^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></span>
It consists of an inner tracking detector surrounded by a thin superconducting solenoid
providing a <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\text{\,}\mathrm{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\text{\,}\mathrm{T}</annotation></semantics></math> axial magnetic field, electromagnetic and hadronic calorimeters, and a muon spectrometer (MS).
The inner tracking detector covers the pseudorapidity range <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\eta|&lt;2.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\eta|&lt;2.5</annotation></semantics></math>.
It consists of silicon pixel, silicon microstrip, and transition radiation tracking detectors.
Lead/liquid-argon (LAr) sampling calorimeters provide electromagnetic (EM) energy measurements
with high granularity within the region <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\eta|&lt;3.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>3.2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\eta|&lt;3.2</annotation></semantics></math>.
A steel/scintillator-tile hadronic calorimeter covers the central pseudorapidity range (<math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\eta|&lt;1.7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1.7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\eta|&lt;1.7</annotation></semantics></math>).
The endcap and forward regions are instrumented with LAr calorimeters
for EM and hadronic energy measurements up to <math id="S2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\eta|=4.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>4.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\eta|=4.9</annotation></semantics></math>.
The MS surrounds the calorimeters and is based on
three large superconducting air-core toroidal magnets with eight coils each.
The field integral of the toroids ranges between <math id="S2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2.0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6.0\text{\,}\mathrm{T}\text{\,}\mathrm{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>6.0</mn><mtext> </mtext><mrow><mi class="ltx_unit">T</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">m</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6.0\text{\,}\mathrm{T}\text{\,}\mathrm{m}</annotation></semantics></math>
across most of the detector.
The MS includes a system of precision tracking chambers up to <math id="S2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\eta|=2.7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2.7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\eta|=2.7</annotation></semantics></math> and fast detectors for triggering up to <math id="S2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\eta|=2.4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2.4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\eta|=2.4</annotation></semantics></math>.
The luminosity is measured mainly by the LUCID–2 detector which is located close to the beampipe <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>.
A two-level trigger system was used to select events <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>.
The first-level trigger is implemented in hardware and used a subset of the detector information
to accept events at a rate close to <math id="S2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100\text{\,}\mathrm{kHz}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>100</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">kHz</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100\text{\,}\mathrm{kHz}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This is followed by a software-based trigger that
reduced the accepted rate of complete events to <math id="S2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\text{\,}\mathrm{kHz}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">kHz</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\text{\,}\mathrm{kHz}</annotation></semantics></math> on average,
depending on the data-taking conditions.
A software suite <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite> is used in data simulation, in the reconstruction
and analysis of real and simulated data, in detector operations, and in the trigger and data acquisition
systems of the experiment.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Data and simulation</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Data and simulated event samples used in this study must satisfy a requirement that all detector systems operate normally <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>. The data were collected with single-electron or single-muon triggers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>.
The <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\textrm{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>T</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\textrm{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> threshold is <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="26\penalty\ (24)\penalty\ \textrm{GeV}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>26</mn><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>24</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>GeV</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">26\penalty\ (24)\penalty\ \textrm{GeV}</annotation></semantics></math> for isolated electron (muon) triggers and <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="60\penalty\ (50)\penalty\ \textrm{GeV}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>60</mn><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>50</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>GeV</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">60\penalty\ (50)\penalty\ \textrm{GeV}</annotation></semantics></math> for non-isolated triggers.
As the analysis targets very high mass QBHs whose decays produce a lepton with <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\textrm{T}}&gt;150" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>T</mtext></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>150</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\textrm{T}}&gt;150</annotation></semantics></math> GeV, virtually all events were triggered by high-<math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\textrm{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>T</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\textrm{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> lepton triggers without isolation requirements whose <math id="S3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\textrm{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>T</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\textrm{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> threshold is <math id="S3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="140\penalty\ (50)\penalty\ \textrm{GeV}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>140</mn><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>50</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>GeV</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">140\penalty\ (50)\penalty\ \textrm{GeV}</annotation></semantics></math> for electrons (muons).
The application of data-quality requirements <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite> results in a sample corresponding to an integrated luminosity of <math id="S3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="164\pm 3\penalty\ \textrm{fb}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>164</mn><mo>±</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mtext>fb</mtext><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">164\pm 3\penalty\ \textrm{fb}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">QBH MC signal samples were generated using the <span id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">QBH v3.02</span> generator <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> to compute the production cross-sections and model the hard-scatter process, assuming zero angular momentum.
Events were then interfaced to <span id="S3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Pythia</span> 8 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite> to model parton showering and hadronisation.
As in previous iterations of searches for QBHs, the CTEQ6L1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite> PDF set was used and the QCD factorisation scale set to the inverse gravitational radius <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>.
Requiring the QBH mass to be in the range <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1{-}3)M_{\textrm{th}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>th</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1{-}3)M_{\textrm{th}}</annotation></semantics></math> (with <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\textrm{th}}=M_{\textrm{D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>th</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>D</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\textrm{th}}=M_{\textrm{D}}</annotation></semantics></math>) ensures that QBHs are produced in a region where quantum effects are significant, excluding thermal decays.
In the ADD model, <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=2,4," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=2,4,</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math> EDs are assumed, whereas <math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=1</annotation></semantics></math> is used in the RS model.
The kinematic distributions of the decay products are governed by the QBH mass; consequently, the signal acceptance and reconstruction efficiency are found to be independent of the specific model type or the number of extra dimensions.
The production cross-section is significantly lower for the RS model than for the ADD model. Samples were produced in <math id="S3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.5\penalty\ \textrm{TeV}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.5</mn><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>TeV</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.5\penalty\ \textrm{TeV}</annotation></semantics></math> steps through threshold mass ranges of <math id="S3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\textrm{th}}\in[6,8.5]\penalty\ \textrm{TeV}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>th</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8.5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>TeV</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\textrm{th}}\in[6,8.5]\penalty\ \textrm{TeV}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[8,10.5]\penalty\ \textrm{TeV}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10.5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>TeV</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[8,10.5]\penalty\ \textrm{TeV}</annotation></semantics></math> for the RS and ADD models, respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Background events from <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>+ ​jets, <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> ​+ ​jets and <math id="S3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="VV\penalty\ (WW,\penalty\ ZZ,\penalty\ WZ)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">VV\penalty\ (WW,\penalty\ ZZ,\penalty\ WZ)</annotation></semantics></math> production were modelled with the <span id="S3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Sherpa</span> 2.2.14  generator <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite> using the <span id="S3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">NNPDF3.0nnlo</span>  PDF set <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite>.
The <math id="S3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\bar{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\bar{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and single-top processes were modelled using the <span id="S3.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Powheg Box</span> v2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite> generator at NLO with the <span id="S3.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">NNPDF3.0nlo</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite> PDF set.
The <math id="S3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\bar{t}W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\bar{t}W</annotation></semantics></math> process was modelled using <span id="S3.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Sherpa</span> 2.2.14, while other <math id="S3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\bar{t}V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\bar{t}V</annotation></semantics></math> samples were produced with <span id="S3.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">MadGraph5_aMC@NLO</span> 2.3.3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite> interfaced to <span id="S3.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Pythia</span> 8 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite>.
Dijet MC samples were produced with <span id="S3.p3.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Pythia</span> 8 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>.
<span id="S3.p3.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">EvtGen</span> 2.1.1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite> was used to perform bottom- and charm-quark decays except for the backgrounds modelled using <span id="S3.p3.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Sherpa</span>, for which the decays were performed internally.
Non-prompt electrons are removed from all MC samples to avoid double-counting their contribution, which is obtained from data.
For the muon channel, this background, estimated from dijet MC events, is negligible.
The same dijet MC sample is used in the electron channel to cross-check the data-driven fake-background estimate.
All generated MC events undergo the full ATLAS detector simulation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite> using <span id="S3.p3.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Geant4</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite>.
The fully simulated MC events are reconstructed in the same manner as the data events and corrected to match the number of interactions per bunch crossing (in-time pile-up) observed in data.
The effect of multiple interactions in the same and neighbouring bunch crossings (pile-up) was modelled by overlaying <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>]</cite> the simulated hard-scattering event
with inelastic proton–proton events generated from a mix of <span id="S3.p3.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Epos</span> 2.0.1.4 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite> and <span id="S3.p3.1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Pythia</span> 8.308 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Event selection and background estimation</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The physics objects used to reconstruct the lepton+jet final states in this analysis include electrons, muons, jets, and missing transverse momentum.
The definitions of these objects as well as the event selection criteria are given below.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Signal electron candidates are reconstructed from energy clusters in the electromagnetic (EM) calorimeter that are matched to an inner-detector track.
They are required to have <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\eta|&lt;2.47" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2.47</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\eta|&lt;2.47</annotation></semantics></math>, excluding the transition region <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.37&lt;|\eta|&lt;1.52" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.37</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1.52</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.37&lt;|\eta|&lt;1.52</annotation></semantics></math> between the barrel and endcap EM calorimeters.
To ensure that these electrons originate from the primary vertex, the longitudinal impact parameter <math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> of their track relative to the primary vertex must satisfy <math id="S4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|z_{0}\sin\theta|&lt;0.5\penalty\ \textrm{mm}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>0.5</mn><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>mm</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|z_{0}\sin\theta|&lt;0.5\penalty\ \textrm{mm}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> is the track’s polar angle.
Similarly, their transverse impact parameter <math id="S4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> must have a significance satisfying <math id="S4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|d_{0}|/\sigma(d_{0})\leq 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|d_{0}|/\sigma(d_{0})\leq 5</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(d_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(d_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> is the uncertainty in <math id="S4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Electrons are required to have <math id="S4.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\textrm{T}}&gt;150" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>T</mtext></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>150</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\textrm{T}}&gt;150</annotation></semantics></math> GeV and satisfy the <span id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Tight</span> identification and <span id="S4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">HighPtCaloOnly</span> isolation criteria <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>]</cite>.
They must also match the corresponding trigger-level electron object.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Signal muon candidates are reconstructed by matching inner-detector tracks in the region <math id="S4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\eta|&lt;2.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\eta|&lt;2.5</annotation></semantics></math> to tracks reconstructed in the MS.
For muons, the transverse impact parameter requirement is tightened to <math id="S4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|d_{0}|/\sigma(d_{0})\leq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|d_{0}|/\sigma(d_{0})\leq 3</annotation></semantics></math>, whereas the longitudinal one is the same as for electrons.
Muons must satisfy <math id="S4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\textrm{T}}&gt;150" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>T</mtext></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>150</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\textrm{T}}&gt;150</annotation></semantics></math> GeV and also the <span id="S4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">High<math id="S4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{-}p_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{-}p_{T}</annotation></semantics></math></span> identification criterion and <span id="S4.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">PflowTightVarRad</span> isolation requirement <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>]</cite>.
This identification criterion is used to ensure optimal muon momentum resolution at high <math id="S4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\textrm{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>T</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\textrm{T}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
It requires at least three hits in each of three layers of precision tracking chambers in the MS, while specific regions of the MS where the alignment is suboptimal are vetoed.
Selected muons are also required to match the trigger-level muon objects.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Jets are reconstructed from particle-flow objects <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>]</cite> using the anti-<math id="S4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> algorithm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx55" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>]</cite> with a radius parameter of <math id="S4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=0.4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=0.4</annotation></semantics></math>.
All jets are required to have <math id="S4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\eta|&lt;2.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\eta|&lt;2.5</annotation></semantics></math>.
Signal jets must satisfy <math id="S4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\textrm{T}}&gt;130\penalty\ \textrm{GeV}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>T</mtext></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>130</mn><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>GeV</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\textrm{T}}&gt;130\penalty\ \textrm{GeV}</annotation></semantics></math>.
To resolve ambiguities between reconstructed objects, a standard overlap-removal procedure is applied to leptons and jets to avoid the double-counting of energy deposits <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The missing transverse momentum, <math id="S4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{\textrm{miss}}_{\textrm{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mtext>T</mtext><mtext>miss</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{\textrm{miss}}_{\textrm{T}}</annotation></semantics></math>, is defined as the magnitude of the negative vectorial sum of the transverse momenta of all selected and calibrated physics objects and other inner-detector tracks from the primary vertex that are not associated with any of these objects <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx56" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>]</cite>.
For the <math id="S4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{\textrm{miss}}_{\textrm{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mtext>T</mtext><mtext>miss</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{\textrm{miss}}_{\textrm{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> calculation, the identification criteria for electrons and muons are relaxed. Additionally, an object-based missing transverse momentum significance <math id="S4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}(p^{\textrm{miss}}_{\textrm{T}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mtext>T</mtext><mtext>miss</mtext></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}(p^{\textrm{miss}}_{\textrm{T}})</annotation></semantics></math> measures the likelihood that the reconstructed <math id="S4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{\textrm{miss}}_{\textrm{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mtext>T</mtext><mtext>miss</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{\textrm{miss}}_{\textrm{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> originates from real invisible particles rather than detector effects.
It is defined as <math id="S4.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}(p^{\textrm{miss}}_{\textrm{T}})=p^{\textrm{miss}}_{\textrm{T}}/\sqrt{\sigma^{2}_{\textrm{L}}(1-\rho^{2}_{\textrm{LT}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mtext>T</mtext><mtext>miss</mtext></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mtext>T</mtext><mtext>miss</mtext></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mtext>L</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mtext>LT</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}(p^{\textrm{miss}}_{\textrm{T}})=p^{\textrm{miss}}_{\textrm{T}}/\sqrt{\sigma^{2}_{\textrm{L}}(1-\rho^{2}_{\textrm{LT}})}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\textrm{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mtext>L</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\textrm{L}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the resolution longitudinally to <math id="S4.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{\textrm{miss}}_{\textrm{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mtext>T</mtext><mtext>miss</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{\textrm{miss}}_{\textrm{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{\textrm{LT}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mtext>LT</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{\textrm{LT}}</annotation></semantics></math> the correlation between the transverse and longitudinal resolutions relative to <math id="S4.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{\textrm{miss}}_{\textrm{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mtext>T</mtext><mtext>miss</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{\textrm{miss}}_{\textrm{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx56" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>]</cite>.
To suppress contributions from pile-up, a jet vertex-tagging <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx57" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>]</cite> requirement is applied to jets with <math id="S4.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\textrm{T}}&lt;60\penalty\ \textrm{GeV}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>T</mtext></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>60</mn><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>GeV</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\textrm{T}}&lt;60\penalty\ \textrm{GeV}</annotation></semantics></math>, which may still enter the analysis via the overlap removal or the calculation of <math id="S4.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{\textrm{miss}}_{\textrm{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mtext>T</mtext><mtext>miss</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{\textrm{miss}}_{\textrm{T}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p6.1" class="ltx_p">All reconstructed objects in simulations have their reconstruction efficiency, energy scale and energy resolution corrected to data, where the corrections discussed in Refs. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx57" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx56" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>]</cite> are updated to the Run 3 conditions.
Moreover, all events are required to contain a primary vertex built from at least two associated tracks with <math id="S4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\textrm{T}}&gt;0.5\penalty\ \textrm{GeV}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>T</mtext></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>0.5</mn><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>GeV</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\textrm{T}}&gt;0.5\penalty\ \textrm{GeV}</annotation></semantics></math>.
If more than one such vertex is present, the primary vertex is the one with the highest sum of squared transverse momenta <math id="S4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma p^{2}_{\textrm{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mtext>T</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma p^{2}_{\textrm{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> of its associated tracks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx58" title="" class="ltx_ref">58</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Candidate events in the signal region (SR) require exactly one signal lepton and at least one signal jet with invariant mass <math id="S4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{\ell j}&gt;3\penalty\ \textrm{TeV}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>TeV</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{\ell j}&gt;3\penalty\ \textrm{TeV}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The angular separation between the lepton and the jet is required to satisfy <math id="S4.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\phi_{\ell j}&gt;2.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2.8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\phi_{\ell j}&gt;2.8</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\eta_{\ell j}&lt;3.25" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>3.25</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\eta_{\ell j}&lt;3.25</annotation></semantics></math> to exploit the back-to-back topology of the QBH decay.
To estimate the shape and/or normalisation of the dominant backgrounds, the analysis defines several control and validation regions (CRs and VRs, respectively).
The normalisation of contributions from <math id="S4.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> events is estimated in single-bin CRs (<math id="S4.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>CR and <math id="S4.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>CR), while their shapes are taken from MC simulation, following confirmation that no shape correction from data is needed.
These estimates are validated in the respective validation regions (<math id="S4.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>VR and <math id="S4.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>VR), by examining the ratios of the total background to data and checking that they are within the uncertainty.
Smaller backgrounds from <math id="S4.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\bar{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\bar{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, single-top, <math id="S4.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="VV" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">VV</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\bar{t}V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\bar{t}V</annotation></semantics></math> events are estimated directly from MC simulation.
The background due to fake/non-prompt electrons is estimated from data by using the matrix method (MM) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx59" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>]</cite>; the rates determined in a dedicated fake-enriched CR (<math id="S4.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>CR) are applied to all other analysis regions to obtain the fake-electron background contribution there.
The behaviour of this background is examined in a corresponding VR (<math id="S4.p7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>VR).
In the muon channel, the corresponding contribution is estimated from the dijet MC sample and found to be negligible.
All CRs and VRs are orthogonal to the SRs and to each other via selections on the <math id="S4.p7.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{\ell j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{\ell j}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p7.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}(p^{\textrm{miss}}_{\textrm{T}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mtext>T</mtext><mtext>miss</mtext></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}(p^{\textrm{miss}}_{\textrm{T}})</annotation></semantics></math> variables.
The region definitions and event selection are summarised in Table <a href="#S4.T1" title='Table 1 ‣ 4 Event selection and background estimation ‣ Search for quantum black holes in lepton+jet final states using proton–proton collisions at √𝑠=13.6⁢"TeV" with the ATLAS detector' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
The final background estimate is obtained from a simultaneous fit to data in the aforementioned regions.
The fit procedure is described in Section <a href="#S6" title='6 Results ‣ Search for quantum black holes in lepton+jet final states using proton–proton collisions at √𝑠=13.6⁢"TeV" with the ATLAS detector' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T1" class="ltx_table">
