<div id="S4.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p8.1" class="ltx_p">The <math id="S4.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>CR is designed to be maximally pure in the <math id="S4.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>+ ​jets background component by requiring <math id="S4.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}(p^{\textrm{miss}}_{\textrm{T}})&gt;5.0\,(3.0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mtext>T</mtext><mtext>miss</mtext></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>5.0</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3.0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}(p^{\textrm{miss}}_{\textrm{T}})&gt;5.0\,(3.0)</annotation></semantics></math> for the electron (muon) channel.
The different selection values originate from the need to accommodate the <math id="S4.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>CR in the electron channel, as discussed below.
Similarly, the <math id="S4.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>CR is made maximally pure in the <math id="S4.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> ​+ ​jets component by requiring a second same-flavour lepton, with the dilepton invariant mass constrained to the range <math id="S4.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="60" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>60</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">60</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S4.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="120\penalty\ \textrm{GeV}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>120</mn><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>GeV</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">120\penalty\ \textrm{GeV}</annotation></semantics></math>. These two CRs are defined in the <math id="S4.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{\ell j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{\ell j}</annotation></semantics></math> range from <math id="S4.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\penalty\ \textrm{TeV}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>TeV</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\penalty\ \textrm{TeV}</annotation></semantics></math>, while the respective VRs cover the <math id="S4.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\penalty\ \textrm{TeV}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>TeV</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\penalty\ \textrm{TeV}</annotation></semantics></math> range.
Distributions in the <math id="S4.p8.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>VRs are shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F1" title='Figure 1 ‣ 4 Event selection and background estimation ‣ Search for quantum black holes in lepton+jet final states using proton–proton collisions at √𝑠=13.6⁢"TeV" with the ATLAS detector' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, where it is shown that data and background predictions agree well within the assigned uncertainties.</p>
