<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">((b)) </span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Invariant mass of the lepton–jet pair in the <math id="S4.F1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>+ ​jets validation region for (a) the electron channel and (b) the muon channel. The data-to-background ratio is shown in the bottom panels, and hatched bands indicate the total statistical and systematic uncertainty. In (a), <span id="S4.F1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“Others”</span> denotes subdominant backgrounds including top and diboson processes. The MC contributions are normalised based on their expected cross-section, prior to the likelihood fit.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p9.1" class="ltx_p">In the electron channel, the dominant background in the SR arises from fakes, estimated via the MM.
The MM requires the definition of <span id="S4.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“real”</span> and <span id="S4.p9.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“fake”</span> rates, <math id="S4.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.
These probabilities are defined as the ratio of events coming from two different electron populations: signal and baseline.
The signal criteria are defined above.
The baseline criteria are defined by relaxing the electron identification from <span id="S4.p9.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Tight</span> to <span id="S4.p9.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Medium</span> and removing the isolation requirement <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>]</cite>.
These criteria are only used in order to (i) estimate <math id="S4.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>CR, and (ii) build the respective background template in the different regions.
The estimation of <math id="S4.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> is performed in a <span id="S4.p9.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“real”</span>-enriched region using <math id="S4.p9.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="Z(\rightarrow e^{+}e^{-})\;\!+\;\!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><msup><mi>e</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.110em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z(\rightarrow e^{+}e^{-})\;\!+\;\!</annotation></semantics></math>jets MC events.
The estimation of <math id="S4.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is performed in a <span id="S4.p9.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“fake”</span>-enriched region that is both orthogonal to the SR and contains a high-enough event yield.
This is achieved by requiring that <math id="S4.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{\ell j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{\ell j}</annotation></semantics></math> be in the range <math id="S4.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S4.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\penalty\ \textrm{TeV}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>TeV</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\penalty\ \textrm{TeV}</annotation></semantics></math>.
To increase the fakes’ purity in that region, contributions from the dominant <math id="S4.p9.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>+ ​jets and <math id="S4.p9.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> ​+ ​jets backgrounds are reduced by placing a requirement on <math id="S4.p9.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}(p^{\textrm{miss}}_{\textrm{T}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mtext>T</mtext><mtext>miss</mtext></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}(p^{\textrm{miss}}_{\textrm{T}})</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S4.p9.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{ee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{ee}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.
The resulting fraction of events with fakes in the <math id="S4.p9.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>CR ranges from <math id="S4.p9.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="60\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>60</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">60\%</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.p9.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="90\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>90</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">90\%</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S4.p9.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="30\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>30</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">30\%</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.p9.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="45\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>45</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">45\%</annotation></semantics></math>) for the baseline (signal) population.
Both <math id="S4.p9.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p9.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> are calculated as a function of the electron <math id="S4.p9.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\textrm{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>T</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\textrm{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> in the range 150–2000 GeV and <math id="S4.p9.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\eta|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\eta|</annotation></semantics></math> in four bins within the range 0–2.47, excluding the transition region mentioned above.
In the calculation of <math id="S4.p9.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>, the prompt-electron contributions found in MC simulation are subtracted from the data in the <math id="S4.p9.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>CR, for both populations separately.
Using the MM, <math id="S4.p9.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p9.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> are combined into weights that are applied to data events meeting the baseline criteria.
This operation yields the fakes background template in the different regions.
The result is validated in the <math id="S4.p9.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>VR, defined in Table <a href="#S4.T1" title='Table 1 ‣ 4 Event selection and background estimation ‣ Search for quantum black holes in lepton+jet final states using proton–proton collisions at √𝑠=13.6⁢"TeV" with the ATLAS detector' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
The <math id="S4.p9.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{\ell j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{\ell j}</annotation></semantics></math> distribution in the <math id="S4.p9.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>VR is shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title='Figure 2 ‣ 4 Event selection and background estimation ‣ Search for quantum black holes in lepton+jet final states using proton–proton collisions at √𝑠=13.6⁢"TeV" with the ATLAS detector' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, where the data and background predictions are seen to be in agreement within the assigned uncertainties.
As can be seen, the fakes component is subdominant in this region, roughly 2–3 times smaller than the <math id="S4.p9.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>+ ​jets component.
Nevertheless, this still allows to assess the modelling of the fakes background estimation there.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2604.19495v2/fig_02.svg" id="S4.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:286/322;" width="286" height="322"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Invariant mass of the lepton–jet pair in the fakes validation region. The data-to-background ratio is shown in the bottom panel, and hatched bands indicate the total statistical and systematic uncertainty. <span id="S4.F2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“Others”</span> denotes subdominant backgrounds including top and diboson processes. The MC contributions are normalised based on their expected cross-section, prior to the likelihood fit.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p10.1" class="ltx_p">In the electron channel prior to the fit, the fakes component constitutes approximately <math id="S4.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="56\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>56</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">56\%</annotation></semantics></math> of the total SR background, followed by <math id="S4.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>+ ​jets at <math id="S4.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="32\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>32</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">32\%</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S4.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> ​+ ​jets, top, and diboson processes contributing the remaining <math id="S4.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>12</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12\%</annotation></semantics></math>.
In the muon channel, <math id="S4.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>+ ​jets dominates at approximately <math id="S4.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="80\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>80</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">80\%</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S4.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> ​+ ​jets, top, and diboson processes accounting for the remaining <math id="S4.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>20</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20\%</annotation></semantics></math>.
The signal acceptance times efficiency ranges from <math id="S4.p10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="76\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>76</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">76\%</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.p10.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="86\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>86</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">86\%</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S4.p10.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="52\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>52</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">52\%</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.p10.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="58\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>58</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">58\%</annotation></semantics></math>) for the electron (muon) channel, for the signals considered in this analysis.
The factors responsible for the difference in yield between the two channels are the lepton acceptance times efficiency and the lepton momentum resolution.
The muon trigger has a lower geometric coverage than the electron counterpart.
Furthermore, very tight identification requirements are necessary to maintain a reasonable muon momentum resolution at high <math id="S4.p10.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\textrm{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>T</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\textrm{T}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Uncertainties associated with the modelling of high-<math id="S4.p10.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\textrm{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>T</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\textrm{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> muon identification are taken into account as discussed in Ref. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>]</cite>.
The limited momentum resolution leads to a significantly worse muon+jet mass resolution as compared to the electron+jet case and thus broader signal shapes.
The lepton+jet mass resolution in the electron channel is consistently below 5% for all signal <math id="S4.p10.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\textrm{th}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>th</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\textrm{th}}</annotation></semantics></math> points considered in the analysis.
The muon-channel mass resolution varies between 25% and 30% for signal <math id="S4.p10.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\textrm{th}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>th</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\textrm{th}}</annotation></semantics></math> values between <math id="S4.p10.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6\penalty\ \textrm{TeV}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>TeV</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6\penalty\ \textrm{TeV}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p10.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10\penalty\ \textrm{TeV}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>TeV</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10\penalty\ \textrm{TeV}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.
The resolution is obtained from the quadrature sum of the standard deviation and the small bias in the mean of the respective lepton+jet mass reconstruction response distribution.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Systematic uncertainties</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Systematic uncertainties affecting the signal and background expectations arise from various experimental and theoretical sources and are evaluated using the methods described below.
Theoretical uncertainties are only calculated for the two leading MC backgrounds, <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>+ ​jets and <math id="S5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>+ ​jets, since the others are significantly smaller and their uncertainties have negligible impact on the analysis.
These uncertainties, which originate from missing higher-order corrections, contribute via the choice of QCD renormalisation and factorisation scales, strong coupling constant and PDFs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite>.
These sources are collectively referred to as <span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“theory uncertainties”</span> and amount to <math id="S5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sim}30\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mn>30</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sim}30\%</annotation></semantics></math> of the <math id="S5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>+ ​jets background yield in the SR.
Following previous publications, no theoretical uncertainties are assigned to the QBH benchmark signals.
This choice is related to the unclarity in the application of these uncertainties to QBH production.
It originates from the lack of an established quantum theory for the interaction between gravity and partons.
Experimental uncertainties are evaluated for all MC signal and background samples.
These uncertainties include those estimated by varying the reconstruction, identification, isolation and trigger efficiencies, and the energy/momentum calibration of electrons <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx60" title="" class="ltx_ref">60</a>]</cite>, muons <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>]</cite>, jets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx61" title="" class="ltx_ref">61</a>]</cite> and <math id="S5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{\textrm{miss}}_{\textrm{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mtext>T</mtext><mtext>miss</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{\textrm{miss}}_{\textrm{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx56" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>]</cite>, as well as uncertainties in the integrated luminosity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx62" title="" class="ltx_ref">62</a>]</cite> and pile-up.
The theory and experimental uncertainties are propagated to the fakes background through the prompt-electron MC event yield that is subtracted from data in the <math id="S5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>CR, as explained in the previous section.
Collectively, they correspond to an uncertainty of approximately 18% of the fakes background in the SR, with the dominant contribution being from QCD scale uncertainties.
Additionally, a separate normalisation uncertainty due to the fakes in the <math id="S5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>CR and SR not having the same mix of origins is considered.
In this analysis, almost all fakes originate from light-flavour decays or photon conversions, according to the dijet MC sample.
The two origins populate the <math id="S5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>CR and SR differently.
Light-flavour decays account for 92% (71%) of fakes in the <math id="S5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>CR (SR), whereas photon conversions account for 8% (29%).
The corresponding uncertainty is found by calculating the weighted average of the difference in the two origins between the <math id="S5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>CR and SR, as follows: let <math id="S5.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{i,j}_{u,v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{i,j}_{u,v}</annotation></semantics></math> be the fraction of electrons of origin <math id="S5.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\text{light-flavour decays, photon conversion}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mtext>light-flavour decays, photon conversion</mtext><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\text{light-flavour decays, photon conversion}\}</annotation></semantics></math> in region <math id="S5.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{f\text{CR, SR}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>CR, SR</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{f\text{CR, SR}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where LF denotes light-flavour decays and <math id="S5.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-conv denotes photon conversions, and <math id="S5.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{\text{LF}}_{\text{SR}}+g^{\gamma\text{-conv}}_{\text{SR}}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mtext>SR</mtext><mtext>LF</mtext></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mtext>SR</mtext><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-conv</mtext></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{\text{LF}}_{\text{SR}}+g^{\gamma\text{-conv}}_{\text{SR}}=1</annotation></semantics></math>.
The uncertainty is therefore <math id="S5.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{\text{LF}}_{\text{SR}}|g^{\text{LF}}_{f\text{CR}}-g^{\text{LF}}_{\text{SR}}|+g^{\gamma\text{-conv}}_{\text{SR}}|g^{\gamma\text{-conv}}_{f\text{CR}}-g^{\gamma\text{-conv}}_{\text{SR}}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mtext>SR</mtext><mtext>LF</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>CR</mtext></mrow><mtext>LF</mtext></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mtext>SR</mtext><mtext>LF</mtext></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mtext>SR</mtext><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-conv</mtext></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>CR</mtext></mrow><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-conv</mtext></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mtext>SR</mtext><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-conv</mtext></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{\text{LF}}_{\text{SR}}|g^{\text{LF}}_{f\text{CR}}-g^{\text{LF}}_{\text{SR}}|+g^{\gamma\text{-conv}}_{\text{SR}}|g^{\gamma\text{-conv}}_{f\text{CR}}-g^{\gamma\text{-conv}}_{\text{SR}}|</annotation></semantics></math>.
This uncertainty amounts to 22% on the yield of the fakes background.
By considering the difference in origin between the <math id="S5.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>CR and <math id="S5.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>VR, the same uncertainty, computed in the same manner, is found to be 17% in the <math id="S5.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>VR.
Lastly, the limited size of the data and MC samples in the <math id="S5.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>CR is accounted for via a separate systematic uncertainty, which is then propagated as a function of electron <math id="S5.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\textrm{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>T</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\textrm{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\eta|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\eta|</annotation></semantics></math> to the SR, where it amounts to about 16%.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Results</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A signal-plus-background model is used to fit the observed <math id="S6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{\ell j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{\ell j}</annotation></semantics></math> spectra simultaneously in the SR, <math id="S6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>CR and <math id="S6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>CR using the profile-likelihood method <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx63" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>]</cite>.
The expected and observed event counts in each <math id="S6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{\ell j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{\ell j}</annotation></semantics></math> bin are described by Poisson-distributed variables, where the uncertainties are accounted for via nuisance parameters.
The SR bins are chosen such that all have a background expectation of <math id="S6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq\mathcal{O}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq\mathcal{O}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> events and are coarser than the detector resolution.
The sensitivity of the analysis is driven by the highest <math id="S6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{\ell j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{\ell j}</annotation></semantics></math> bin of the SR, 5–13 TeV.
Contributions above 13 TeV are included in this bin.
In that bin, the number of background events in the electron channel is predicted to be <math id="S6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.46\pm 0.65_{\textrm{stat.}}\pm 0.38_{\textrm{syst.}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1.46</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">±</mo><msub><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0.65</mn><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">stat.</mtext></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">±</mo><msub><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0.38</mn><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">syst.</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.46\pm 0.65_{\textrm{stat.}}\pm 0.38_{\textrm{syst.}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where the leading systematic uncertainty is from the fakes estimation.
In the muon channel, the number of background events in the highest bin is predicted to be <math id="S6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.10\pm 0.95_{\textrm{stat.}}\pm 0.47_{\textrm{syst.}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">3.10</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">±</mo><msub><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0.95</mn><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">stat.</mtext></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">±</mo><msub><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0.47</mn><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">syst.</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.10\pm 0.95_{\textrm{stat.}}\pm 0.47_{\textrm{syst.}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where the leading systematic uncertainty is due to the modelling of the <math id="S6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>+ ​jets background.
Normalisation factors for the leading MC backgrounds, <math id="S6.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>+ ​jets and <math id="S6.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> ​+ ​jets, are found to be <math id="S6.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.2\pm 0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.2</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.2\pm 0.5</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.1\pm 0.4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.1</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.1\pm 0.4</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S6.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.1\pm 0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.1</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.1\pm 0.5</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.0\pm 0.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.0</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.0\pm 0.3</annotation></semantics></math>) for <math id="S6.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>+ ​jets and <math id="S6.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> ​+ ​jets in the electron (muon) channel, respectively.
A moderate 20% (25%) non-closure is observed in the <math id="S6.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>VR in the electron (muon) channel; given the small <math id="S6.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> ​+ ​jets fraction of 5% (12%) in the SR, the potential impact on the background yield in the SR is at most 1%–2% and is negligible. It is therefore not included in the fit as a separate uncertainty.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">No significant excess of data is observed above the SM background.
The <math id="S6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{\ell j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{\ell j}</annotation></semantics></math> distributions in the electron channel SR and muon channel SR after the fit are shown in Figure <a href="#S6.F3" title='Figure 3 ‣ 6 Results ‣ Search for quantum black holes in lepton+jet final states using proton–proton collisions at √𝑠=13.6⁢"TeV" with the ATLAS detector' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.
Benchmark signal MC predictions corresponding to the RS and ADD models are overlaid for reference.
The signals considered in this search are generally expected to populate the <math id="S6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{\ell j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{\ell j}</annotation></semantics></math> region above 6 TeV, while the background shape falls steeply between 3 and 6 TeV in both channels.
In the electron channel, the highest-mass event observed in data corresponds to <math id="S6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{\ell j}=5.3\penalty\ \textrm{TeV}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>5.3</mn><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>TeV</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{\ell j}=5.3\penalty\ \textrm{TeV}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This event features an electron with <math id="S6.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p_{\textrm{T}},\eta)=(1.2\penalty\ \textrm{TeV},1.55)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>T</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1.2</mn><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>TeV</mtext></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1.55</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p_{\textrm{T}},\eta)=(1.2\penalty\ \textrm{TeV},1.55)</annotation></semantics></math>, a jet with <math id="S6.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p_{\textrm{T}},\eta)=(1.2\penalty\ \textrm{TeV},-1.29)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>T</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1.2</mn><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>TeV</mtext></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1.29</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p_{\textrm{T}},\eta)=(1.2\penalty\ \textrm{TeV},-1.29)</annotation></semantics></math>, and a low <math id="S6.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}(p_{\textrm{T}}^{\textrm{miss}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mtext>T</mtext><mtext>miss</mtext></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}(p_{\textrm{T}}^{\textrm{miss}})</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S6.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.80" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2.80</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.80</annotation></semantics></math>.
The objects are nearly back-to-back in the transverse plane with <math id="S6.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\phi(\ell,j)=3.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>3.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\phi(\ell,j)=3.05</annotation></semantics></math>, with a large pseudorapidity separation <math id="S6.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\eta(\ell,j)=2.84" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2.84</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\eta(\ell,j)=2.84</annotation></semantics></math>.
In the muon channel, the event with the highest invariant mass corresponds to <math id="S6.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{\ell j}=5.5\penalty\ \textrm{TeV}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>5.5</mn><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>TeV</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{\ell j}=5.5\penalty\ \textrm{TeV}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This event features a muon with <math id="S6.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p_{\textrm{T}},\eta)=(5.2\penalty\ \textrm{TeV},1.33)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>T</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>5.2</mn><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>TeV</mtext></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1.33</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p_{\textrm{T}},\eta)=(5.2\penalty\ \textrm{TeV},1.33)</annotation></semantics></math>, a jet with <math id="S6.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p_{\textrm{T}},\eta)=(1.2\penalty\ \textrm{TeV},0.39)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>T</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1.2</mn><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>TeV</mtext></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0.39</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p_{\textrm{T}},\eta)=(1.2\penalty\ \textrm{TeV},0.39)</annotation></semantics></math>, and a low <math id="S6.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}(p_{\textrm{T}}^{\textrm{miss}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mtext>T</mtext><mtext>miss</mtext></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}(p_{\textrm{T}}^{\textrm{miss}})</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S6.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.43" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1.43</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.43</annotation></semantics></math>.
The objects are nearly back-to-back in the transverse plane with <math id="S6.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\phi(\ell,j)=3.11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>3.11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\phi(\ell,j)=3.11</annotation></semantics></math>, with a relatively small pseudorapidity separation of <math id="S6.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\eta(\ell,j)=0.94" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.94</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\eta(\ell,j)=0.94</annotation></semantics></math> indicating a centrally produced system.</p>
</div>
